id: pm-999jh7
title: "list applies an undisclosed default lifecycle scope and reports the scoped count as the corpus total with has_more false, while search applies no such scope, so the two discovery surfaces disagree about what exists"
description: "Measured on 2026-08-03 against the real tracker using pm 2026.8.3.\n\npm list --type Issue --limit 900 returns 127 rows and an envelope stating total 127 and has_more false. The workspace holds 722 items of type Issue; pm stats reports 719 and pm list --type Issue --status all --limit 900 returns 722. So the command answers the question 'how many Issues does this project have' with a number that is 17 percent of the truth, and attaches an explicit assertion that nothing more exists.\n\nThree separate contract failures compose here, and only the first is documented:\n\n1. A default lifecycle scope is applied. --help does say 'use all for no status filter', which implies a default exists, but never states what it is. That is a documentation gap on its own.\n2. The filters block does not disclose it. filters is the envelope's mechanism for stating what was applied; it reports {type, limit, runtime_filters:{}} and omits the filter that removed 595 rows. A consumer cannot reconstruct the query from its own receipt.\n3. total and has_more describe the scoped set as if it were the whole set. The project has already established the principle that omission must be receipted - the omission_receipt block exists precisely so a caller knows fields were withheld and how to restore them. Row-level omission by default scope has no equivalent, and worse, the two fields that would carry the signal both actively deny it.\n\nThe surfaces also disagree with each other. pm search 'release' --limit 900 returns 869 rows with total null, and returns the identical 869 with --status all, so search applies no default lifecycle scope at all. An agent that orients with list sees active work; an agent that orients with search sees the full history; neither is told which it is looking at. A closed story once argued search should scope to open work by default; list scopes and search does not, and nothing records that this divergence is intentional.\n\nWhy this matters more than a default. This project's operating rules require an agent to search existing items and reuse the canonical lineage before creating work, and the single largest source of duplicate work is failing to see terminal items. The default that hides 82 percent of the Issue corpus is applied on the exact surface an agent uses to check for prior art, and the envelope tells it the view was complete. Composability compounds it: the documented shell idiom pipes list --json into jq, and a jq expression cannot second-guess a total the envelope asserts.\n\nScale symmetry: the failure is a property of the corpus's terminal fraction, not its size. At 2,285 items the hidden fraction is 82 percent of Issues; in a decades-long workspace it approaches 100 percent, and the surface will confidently report a few hundred rows as the entire project.\n\nScope is default-scope disclosure and the meaning of total and has_more on row-producing reads. The absence of a shared row contract across read surfaces has its own owner; this is what the envelope must say once that contract exists."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[6]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:contracts",context-management,data-quality,scale
